    "title": "What Rick Barnes said about Bruce Pearl retiring at Auburn, Steven Pearl's hiring",
    "text": "Updated Sept. 23, 2025, 3:53 p.m. ETFormer Tennessee and Auburn coach Bruce Pearl announced his retirement from coaching.Tennessee coach Rick Barnes expressed his happiness for Pearl and respect for his decision.Pearl's son, Steven, has been named the new head coach for Auburn.Rick Barnes caught up with Bruce Pearl in mid-September.The Tennessee basketball coach left that conversation with an inkling of what was coming for the Auburn coach. That inkling became reality on Sept. 22 as the Tigers coach and former Vols coach announced his retirement.\"I am happy for him,\" Barnes said Sept. 23. \"I really am. What he said and what he meant in his statement from talking to him, he felt it was time. I certainly respect that. You look at him, everywhere he has been, he has built a winning program and has done a great job.\"Pearl retired after 11 years as the Auburn coach, which marked the most successful run in the program's history. He is moving into an ambassador’s role in the athletic department as special assistant to the athletics director.He went 246-125 at Auburn and took the Tigers to Final Fours in 2019 and 2025. Auburn won the SEC regular-season title in 2024-25, one of its four combined SEC titles between the conference tournament and the regular season.Pearl's son, Steven, took over as the head coach for the 2025-26 season after signing a five-year contract.\"I think Steven will do a great job,\" Barnes said. \"You can’t live with someone like Bruce and not understand the game, being around the game. ... I think Steven has worked hard and I think the fact Auburn has given him this opportunity speaks volumes of what they think about him.\"The Vols went 7-9 against Pearl during Barnes' tenure. They won the final matchup 70-65 in the SEC Tournament semifinals in 2025.\"This is something I knew that he wanted,\" Barnes said. \"I am happy for him. I am happy for Steven. There is no doubt he is going to continue to do a great job.”Pearl coached at Tennessee from 2005-11, elevating the program by going 145-61 and reaching the NCAA Tournament in each of his six seasons. He was fired following the 2010-11 season amid NCAA recruiting violations and a charge from the NCAA for unethical conduct for lying during the investigation into the recruiting violations. He received a three-year show-cause penalty in August 2011. Auburn hired Pearl in March 2014.UT reached its first Elite Eight in 2010 under Pearl. It has gone to back-to-back Elite Eights under Barnes in the past two seasons.Pearl also led UT to its first No. 1 ranking, a one-week stint in the 2007-08 season. The Vols were the top-ranked team for four consecutive weeks in the 2018-19 season. They spent a program-record five weeks as the No. 1 team in the 2024-25 season.Pearl had a 708-269 career record, which included stops at Division II Southern Indiana (1992-2001) and Milwaukee (2001-05).Mike Wilson covers University of Tennessee athletics. Email him at michael.wilson@knoxnews.com and follow him on X @ByMikeWilson. If you enjoy Mike’s coverage, consider a digital subscription that will allow you access to all of it.",
